<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles III of Spain</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1716–1788</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Charles of Bourbon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, previously king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, ruled Spain from 1759 to 1788. An enlightened reformer, he promoted roads, trade, academies and royal factories and expelled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuits</a> in 1767. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bourbon-reforms">Bourbon Reforms</a> created the Viceroyalty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a>, the Captaincy General of Venezuela and the Internal Provinces, and introduced intendants.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-iii-of-spain.jpg" alt="Charles III of Spain" width="320" height="448" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anton Raphael Mengs · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charles_III_of_Spain_high_resolution.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
